<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adam Mickiewicz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Adam Bernard Mickiewicz</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adam Mickiewicz was a Polish poet, dramatist, essayist, publicist, translator and political activist, a principal figure of Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> and regarded as a national poet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. Born in 1798 at or near Navahrudak, in lands of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> then under Russian rule, he spent five years exiled to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1829, living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, where he lectured on Slavic literature at the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His poetic drama Dziady (Forefathers' Eve) and the epic Pan Tadeusz inspired uprisings against the partitioning powers. He died, probably of cholera, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> in 1855 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adam-mickiewicz.jpg" alt="Adam Mickiewicz" width="330" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adam_Mickiewicz_według_dagerotypu_paryskiego_z_1842_roku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mickiewicz's father, Mikołaj, was a lawyer and a member of the Polish nobility; his mother was Barbara, née Majewska. He was the second-born son. From 1807 to 1815 he attended a Dominican school and was a mediocre student, and in September 1815 he enrolled at the Imperial University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> to train as a teacher. He taught secondary school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a> from 1819 to 1823. His first poem, Zima miejska, appeared in 1818, and his anthologies of 1822 and 1823, which included Grażyna and the first published parts of Dziady, mark the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1817 Mickiewicz and friends including Tomasz Zan founded the Philomaths, a secret student society focused on self-education. An investigation by Nikolay Novosiltsev led to his arrest, and in 1824 he was banished to central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. He spent most of the next five years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where literary circles prized his talent for improvisation, and a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odessa">Odessa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1825 inspired the Crimean Sonnets. Novosiltsev recognised the subversive message of Konrad Wallenrod, published in 1828, which the Moscow censors had missed.</p><p>Allowed to go abroad in 1829, he met Goethe at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> about October 1830, leaving in April 1831. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 31 July 1832, and Pan Tadeusz appeared there in 1834. That year he married Celina Szymanowska; they had six children. The family lived in relative poverty, and he never received French citizenship. In 1849 he founded the newspaper La Tribune des Peuples, which the authorities shut down on 10 November. In October 1852 he was hired as a librarian at the Bibliothèque de l'Arsenal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died, probably of cholera, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where he had gone to help organise Polish and Jewish forces to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>. His remains were moved in 1894 from Montmorency in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to Wawel Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>. He is counted among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s Three Bards with Zygmunt Krasiński and Juliusz Słowacki, and scholars speak of a &quot;cult of Mickiewicz&quot; for the reverence in which he is held as a &quot;national prophet&quot;.</p><p>His works also promoted the Lithuanian National Revival, and the opening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s national anthem, Tautiška giesmė, is a paraphrase of the beginning of Pan Tadeusz. Museums dedicated to him stand in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, Navahrudak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adam Mickiewicz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Carl Maria von Weber’s opera Der Freischütz triumphed in Berlin in 1821, and in 1822 <b>Adam Mickiewicz</b>’s Ballads and Romances began the Romantic age in Polish literature.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Adam Mickiewicz</b> placed it twice in Pan Tadeusz (1834), and in 1842 he explained its opening idea to his students in Paris.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li></ul></section>
